56:46.820 --> 56:47.260
Exactly.

56:47.260 --> 56:55.380
It makes you realize how unimaginably capable, adaptable, and underutilized the human body truly is in our digital age.

56:55.780 --> 56:59.640
The foundational takeaway is that the body is the first and primary distinction machine.

57:00.180 --> 57:03.500
The keyboard, the mouse, the touchscreen, these are all secondary.

57:04.200 --> 57:11.420
They are merely localized interfaces trying, often poorly, to tap into the body's innate, massive capacity for distinction making.

57:11.420 --> 57:14.360
We have traveled an incredibly long way today.

57:14.880 --> 57:20.140
We started by looking at a piece of plastic on a desk and debunking the persistent illusion of the spatial memory map.

57:20.320 --> 57:21.980
We prove that we don't look up coordinates.

57:22.520 --> 57:25.860
We execute procedural motor chunks based on the identity of our fingers.

57:25.860 --> 57:36.000
We explored how those chunks form a literal motor phonology, mapping the linguistic concepts of place and manner directly onto the physical movements of our hands.

57:36.000 --> 57:46.140
We saw how understanding that biology completely changes the mathematics of hardware design, opening the door for the massive generative compression of a 30-position, 8-letter keyboard.

57:46.340 --> 58:00.240
We examined the canonical gesture, the slingshot arc of the hand, and the tragedy of how current computing systems aggressively discard the rich, continuous flow of our movements in favor of rigid, lossy sequences.

